---
name: rename
description: Take ownership of a session role name on the declared fleet transport — arms the {{SOLET_NAME}} watch registered-presence watcher (default) or re-registers over the optional MCP bridge (when declared) — keeping the platform-side role binding, the address book, and the local Claude Code session label in lock-step. Use on /rename <Role-Name> and on SessionStart re-claim prompts.
---

The user (or the SessionStart re-claim hook) supplied a role name as the
argument — call it `$ROLE` below. Take ownership of it for this session: the
platform-side role binding and address-book entry that back
`peer_send_by_name` routing and scheduled wake-ups, kept in lock-step with
the local Claude Code session label.

This is the seed render of the canonical rename skill; the platform-checkout
render is `.claude/commands/rename.md` in the platform repo. The two bodies
stay in lock-step and differ only in CLI command name, MCP tool prefix, and
the unset-transport default.

**Transport selection — declared, never probed, never silently crossed.**
Read `FLEET_TRANSPORT` from this session's environment (Bash:
`echo "${FLEET_TRANSPORT:-watch}"` — unset means `watch`, the
standing default on hydrated machines).

- `watch` → watcher path below.
- `mcp` → MCP path below (only ever declared where the operator explicitly
  wired the optional MCP bridge at hydration).
- If the declared path's mechanism is missing or fails, surface the exact
  error and stop. Do NOT switch to the other path on your own — the other
  path may be used only when the user explicitly asks for it in this
  conversation. An explicit instruction is not a fallback. In particular, do
  not register, troubleshoot, or suggest MCP to make renaming work when the
  declared transport is `watch`.

## Watcher path (transport `watch`, the default)

**Detect the incumbent watcher FIRST — it decides which of the two branches
below you take.** A fleet-spawned worker is launched with its presence
watcher already armed by the spawn adapter (`--no-claim`: it registered
presence and deliberately claimed no role); an operator-launched session has
no sidecar and must arm its own. Arming a second watcher for the same session
is REFUSED, never merged — `{{SOLET_NAME}} watch` takes a per-session flock
and exits with `another watcher already holds this session's lock ... one
watch per session; stop the incumbent first` — so branch 1b run blindly just
errors out on every spawned worker.

A watcher does NOT register under `$AGENT_INSTANCE_ID`. It registers under a
derived, stable id: `agi-watch-` + the first 24 hex chars of
`sha256($AGENT_SESSION_ID)`. Compute it and look for it in the registry:

```bash
WATCH_ID="agi-watch-$(printf %s "$AGENT_SESSION_ID" | shasum -a 256 | cut -c1-24)"
echo "$WATCH_ID"
{{SOLET_NAME}} call plugin::agent_messaging_plugin::peer_list '{}' | grep -c "$WATCH_ID"
```

- **`$WATCH_ID` present in `peer_list`** (count ≥ 1) → branch 1a.
- **Absent** (count 0) → branch 1b.

### 1a. Incumbent sidecar → claim the role through it (do NOT arm a second)

Presence already exists; only the durable role binding is missing. Claim it
with the verb, against the identity the WATCHER actually registered — never
`$AGENT_INSTANCE_ID`, which is the launcher's id and holds no binding:

```bash
{{SOLET_NAME}} call plugin::agent_messaging_plugin::peer_claim_role \
  "{\"name\": \"$ROLE\", \"agent_id\": \"$AGENT_IDENTITY\", \"agent_instance_id\": \"$WATCH_ID\", \"agent_session_id\": \"$AGENT_SESSION_ID\", \"session_label\": \"$ROLE\"}"
```

Confirm `action` is `registered` (first claim) or `updated` (refresh). If the
call reports `role_held_live`, another live session holds the name — report
that and stop; do not pass `takeover` without operator confirmation. Then go
to step 4, using `$WATCH_ID` wherever it asks for the instance id.

### 1b. No incumbent → arm the watcher with the role, as before

1. Start the registered-presence watcher as a persistent background process
   (a background Bash task; keep it running for the session's lifetime; if a
   previous watcher from this conversation is running for a different role,
   stop it first):
   ```bash
   {{SOLET_NAME}} watch --role "$ROLE"
   ```
   On arming it registers this session in the peer registry (identity from
   the launcher-exported `AGENT_SESSION_ID`), claims `$ROLE` as
   the durable role binding, drains messages that arrived while unwatched,
   then streams one JSON line per delivery — and nothing while idle. It
   reconnects and re-claims automatically across solet restarts and
   blue-green swaps. It also tees each delivery into a per-session spool
   consumed by the `{{SOLET_NAME}} wake` Stop hook (installed at user
   scope by hydration): when this session goes idle, that hook blocks at
   zero token cost and turns the next delivery into a session turn — no MCP,
   and it works the same on any inference provider. If the hook is absent (a
   harness without hook support), deliveries wait in the watch output for
   the next look; that is the floor, not the ceiling. If the watcher exits
   immediately complaining about a missing session id, the session was not
   started via the `claude-{{SOLET_NAME}}` launcher or fleet
   functions — say so rather than inventing an id.

2. Verify from the watcher's first output line: `"watch": "armed"` with the
   claim result (`claimed`/`updated`/`displaced`) and note the
   `agent_instance_id` it printed. New JSON lines on later looks are
   deliveries (peer messages, role-addressed sends) — treat them like any
   peer message.

   Note the `"claim"` value here reports `{"claimed": false, "reason":
   "managed_registration_only"}` for a `--no-claim` arm — that is the
   sidecar's shape, not this branch's; seeing it means you took the wrong
   branch and should claim via 1a instead.

## MCP path (transport `mcp`)

Requires the `mcp__{{SOLET_NAME}}__*` tools in this session (the
operator explicitly wired the optional MCP bridge, and the launcher attaches
it in this mode). If they are absent, that is a launch/configuration error —
report it.

1. Call `mcp__{{SOLET_NAME}}__peer_register` with
   `agent_id="claude_code"` and `session_label="$ROLE"`. Capture BOTH
   `agent_instance_id` AND `parent_pid` from the response.

2. Call `mcp__{{SOLET_NAME}}__process_call` with:
   - `process_key`: `plugin::agent_messaging_plugin::peer_claim_role`
   - `arguments`: `{"name": "$ROLE", "agent_id": "claude_code", "agent_instance_id": "<value from step 1>", "session_label": "$ROLE"}`
   - `reason`: brief explanation of the rename

3. Read the `bridge_delivery_result` channel notification for the
   `process_call` response. Confirm `action` is `registered` (first claim)
   or `updated` (refresh of an existing claim).

## Both paths — finish

4. If the new role differs from the label this session was launched with,
   patch the local Claude Code session label: set the top-level `name` field
   of `~/.claude/sessions/<parent_pid>.json` (MCP path: `parent_pid` from
   step 1; watcher path: the ancestor `claude` process pid, e.g. walk up
   from `$$` with `ps -o ppid= -p`). Use jq with an atomic rename:
   ```bash
   jq --arg name "$ROLE" '.name = $name' ~/.claude/sessions/<parent_pid>.json > /tmp/rename.$$.json && mv /tmp/rename.$$.json ~/.claude/sessions/<parent_pid>.json
   ```
   Skip this step when the role equals the launch label (the SessionStart
   re-claim case) — the file already says the right thing.

   **This patch is the last lock-step action of a completed claim from steps
   1–3 above, never a standalone act.** Writing this label without a
   preceding successful claim does not work around a blocked claim — it
   forges the lock-step invariant this step exists to preserve: a git-mutation
   gate that reads this file checks for the label only, so a label written
   without its claim looks identical to one earned by a real claim while
   holding none of the protection (the platform-side singleton lock, the loud
   refusal on a second live claim) that makes holding the role mean anything.
   If steps 1–3 failed or the declared transport was unavailable, that is the
   "declared path's mechanism is missing or fails" case from the transport
   selection guidance above — the correct response is still to surface the
   error and stop, not to reach this step anyway.

5. Report in one short sentence: which role this session now holds, over
   which transport, and whether it was a fresh claim, a refresh, or a
   displacement (displacement from a stale binding is normal after a restart
   or /clear). The visible terminal session indicator follows the
   launch-time `--name` argument and only changes after restarting via the
   launcher; the platform-side claim is effective immediately and is the one
   that matters for routing.

Notes:
- Ground truth for any claim, on demand:
  `{{SOLET_NAME}} call plugin::agent_messaging_plugin::peer_holds_role`
  with `{"name": "$ROLE", "agent_instance_id": "<from the armed line / step 1>"}`
  — a raw peer-list entry is presence, not a claim.
- If the role is already held by ANOTHER LIVE session, the claim is REFUSED
  with `role_held_live`, naming the incumbent. It does NOT displace the
  holder. Report the refusal and stop; passing `takeover` needs explicit
  operator confirmation first, exactly as branch 1a says. (This paragraph
  used to say the opposite — that the claim "still succeeds and displaces the
  prior holder". That described the behaviour BEFORE the `role_held_live`
  gate shipped, and it contradicted this skill's own branch-1a instruction.)
- A holder that reads DEAD by the liveness window is still claimable, with no
  takeover and no explicit terminate. That is deliberate, not a gap: a
  crashed session cannot release its own role, so refusing dead holders too
  would strand the role permanently behind a session that no longer exists.
- Still only run one session per role name at a time.
- If any step fails, surface the error verbatim — don't paper over it.
